   "created_at": "2026-10-24T05:56:58Z",
   "updated_at": "2026-10-24T05:56:58Z",
   "body": "This comes from magpie's local trust model rather than a missing endpoint. A request from this computer (loopback) needs no key at all — every local agent relies on that — so `GET /v1/models` answers 200 for a local caller whatever the key: right, wrong, or none. A client that verifies an API key by probing a model-list endpoint (which is what Anarlog's message says it's doing) can't distinguish those, and concludes the endpoint \"doesn't support API key verification\". The gateway endpoint itself exists and works; it just doesn't refuse wrong keys for loopback callers, by design (`internal/gateway/lan.go`/`allow.go`: loopback is trusted, and is never taken as key-verified).\n\nWhat works today for key-verifying clients: reach magpie from another machine — the Docker image with `magpie web --addr 0.0.0.0` on a server, or any remote deployment — with Share on local network enabled and a gateway key set. There the same `GET /v1/models` answers 401 for a wrong key and 200 for a right one, exactly what such verification needs.\n\nTwo things that would help pin this down further:\n\n1. Which endpoint does Anarlog probe for verification (`/v1/models`, `/v1/models/{id}`, something else)? If it's one magpie serves differently, that's worth knowing.\n2. If your magpie and Anarlog run on the same machine, the practical ask is on Anarlog's side — a \"skip key verification / trust local endpoint\" option — since magpie keeping loopback keyless is what lets every local agent connect without configuration.",
   "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
